# Kopia mutate

## Gate

Load only when the user authorized Kopia **and** used an explicit mutate
verb this turn (**snapshot**, **backup**, **restore**, **forget**,
**delete**, **maintain** / **expire**) naming the path, snapshot id, or
policy scope. Otherwise stay on `kopia-inventory`. Always pair with
`kopia-safety` before any mutate command.

No Kopia MCP discovered — use `kopia` CLI only. **Do not invent MCP tool
names.**

## Inventory before mutate

1. `kopia repository status` this turn (`kopia-inventory`).
2. Resolve every path and snapshot id from `kopia snapshot list` or user
   paste — never guess.
3. For restore: prove snapshot exists (`kopia snapshot list` / describe)
   before writing files.
4. State planned action + path/destination in one line; then run the command.

## Live CLI mutate map

| Action | Command | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Create snapshot | `kopia snapshot create PATH` | PATH must be named this turn |
| Restore | `kopia restore SNAPSHOT_ID DEST` (or equivalent restore subcommand for the installed CLI) | overwrite-shaped — warn first |
| Forget snapshot | `kopia snapshot delete SNAPSHOT_ID` / `kopia snapshot forget` per installed CLI | explicit **forget**/**delete** + id |
| Policy set | `kopia policy set PATH …` | only fields the user named |
| Maintenance run | `kopia maintenance run` | only when user asked; can expire data |

Confirm the exact subcommand spelling against `kopia --help` / `kopia COMMAND
--help` on the installed binary — flag names vary by version. Prefer
`--json` for receipts when available.

## Defaults

- Snapshot source: only paths the user named — never walk the whole disk.
- Restore destination: require an explicit DEST; refuse silent overwrite of
  home or system roots without naming them.
- Retention / forget: prefer policy-driven expiry the user already set over
  ad-hoc mass forget.
- State which flags you relied on in the receipt.

## Restore warning

Restore can **overwrite live files** at DEST.

1. Warn destination impact in plain language.
2. Prove snapshot id from inventory.
3. Prefer a non-live staging DEST when the user did not insist on in-place
   overwrite.

After restore: list DEST or re-run a narrow inventory check; report paths
touched (counts ok).

## Forget / maintenance discipline

- Forget: explicit verb + snapshot id(s) listed this turn — no "clean up
  old" mass delete without named ids or a named retention policy action.
- Maintenance: say whether full or quick; warn that expired content may be
  removed permanently.

## Operator receipt (mutate)

- action (snapshot / restore / forget / policy / maintenance)
- path or snapshot id(s)
- destination (restore)
- outcome (ok / error summary)
- no passwords, keys, or connection strings

## Related (this plugin)

- `kopia-inventory` — status / list before mutate
- `kopia-safety` — gates, restore risk, credential refuse
